  

<h2 id="thoughts-on-notes-tasks-and-life">Thoughts on notes, tasks, and life</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/8198615d-3c31-43f6-ae1d-ed25cb24e4d2/pasted-percent20image-percent2020260921203013.png" alt="Photograph of an envelope that has a jagged rip along the upper portion. There’s digital painting on top to obscure sensitive information and to decorate the background. There’s a hand drawn heart drawn thickly in the middle of the envelope." width="2380" height="1778"></p>
<p><em>Photo of the envelope in question. Digital painting done with my index finger on my iPhone in the photos app.</em></p>
<hr>
<p>Today a registered letter arrived at my place. It was a conspicuously passport-sized envelope, addressed to my baby grandson. I signed for it, and immediately sent a photo of the envelope with his mother to announce its anticipated arrival.</p>
<p><em>Hooray</em>! she exclaimed. <em>Open it, and show me his dear little face!</em> Done. We bathed in the cuteness. Then she said, <em>It’s his first ever letter, I’ll have to keep the envelope.</em></p>
<p>Oh.</p>
<p>Oh dear.</p>
<p>In my haste, I had torn the envelope open to get to what was inside! I pressed it back together as best I could, took a photo of the damage and sent it to the proud Mama with my apologies.</p>
<p>Her response?</p>
<p><em>Perfect!</em></p>
<p>A grandma’s eagerness to see her tiny grandbaby had apparently become a<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>perfect” expression of love, shaped by the moment. Now that’s what I call a good attitude!</p>
<h3 id="perfection-comes-in-moments-once-your-feet-are-moving">Perfection comes in moments, once your feet are moving</h3>
<p>So — perfection. What is it? And is it worth pursuing?</p>
<p>The 11th Century old French word <em>parfit</em> referred to something that was finished or completed. Something that was ready. The notion of flawlessness, with nothing wanted by way of improvement, emerged later.<a href="#footnote-1J3S" class="footnote-ref" id="ref-1J3S" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a></p>
<p>In my mind, the problem with the word, especially when applied to note making, <span class="small-caps">PKM</span>, or task management is that it seems to imply a static state. Something encased in marble, inflexible in its loveliness.</p>
<p>Can you see the paradox? Something that is<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>complete”, will become the very definition of imperfect when applied to a situation that doesn’t fit the original design.</p>
<p>The complete becomes incomplete. The right becomes wrong.</p>
<p>Perhaps perfection is a path, or a direction, rather than an achievable destination. In this life, at least! That’s a pretty good case for starting rough and refining as you go.</p>
<p>Because starting rough is infinitely better than not starting at all.</p>
<p>Having an incomplete, imperfect system is something to be proud of. Buffeted by life, it will change shape until it fits you as well as any system can. And tomorrow, when things change (you know they will), you’ll make adjustments and keep going.</p>
<h3 id="yeah-but-how">Yeah, but <em>how</em>&nbsp;??</h3>
<p>Stop asking how, and just begin!</p>
<p>Write that task, write that idea. The perfect structure doesn’t need to be in place first. Keep notes on what feels good, and what you’d like to see happen that’s currently lacking. Keep your eyes open for the simplest possible solutions to the issues you identify.</p>
<p>That’s pretty much it.</p>
<p>Perhaps you were hoping for a bit more specific guidance? Maybe another time. The one thing I will strongly suggest here is this:</p>
<p><strong>Keep the raw information you are working with in its own independent sphere</strong>. Fully portable, and readable by any device from any era. Ideally, as plain text files that multiple apps can work on <em>without changing the core information</em>. And, of course, back it up. Then back it up again.</p>
<p>Oh, and don’t trust anything to an app that has no exit! Export everything you have stored in a closed app (one you cannot access your information without) to a format most apps can read, like <code>.txt</code>, <code>.md</code>, <code>.pdf</code>. Do this daily. Multiple times a day, if you’re writing up a storm.</p>
<p>If a specialised app seems a bit complex but it’s been working for you, keep using it! No need to chase someone else’s idea of simplicity. Your intuition is going to serve you much better than anyone else’s self-proclaimed good ideas.</p>
<p>Now go have some delightfully imperfect fun! No expectations. Just write down whatever is in your head and shape it the best way you can. Let go of perfectionism, which I define as the unrealistic expectation of flawlessness, and see what happens!</p>
<p>Pursue excellence, by all means! But don’t fall prey to the kind of perfectionism that makes you afraid to make mistakes. Try celebrating the tiny moments that feel like perfection even when they are messy.</p>
